 >>[2026-10-12 14:26] observe — resolved by code: census, graph, measure, stack are canonical in commands.ts with invent, map, mass/thrift, and surface/profile as aliases, so census_vs_roster, graph_rename, surface_vs_stack, profile_compat, surface_p0 are answered; inv, geom, topo aliases exist (ultra_short). Still open: spw census 'prompts/**' reports 0 files while spw census prompts reports 95, so SelectionIR globs have not landed; no stream or crystallize verb exists.
 >>[2026-10-12 14:27] revise — commits 1-2 moved to done (aliases in commands.ts); commit 3 kept with a concrete glob repro; commit 4 swapped from surface composite to the progressive-product adoption named in the card; open questions cut from 9 to 4 and sharpened. Phase is landed so no wonders added.
 >>[2026-10-12 14:52] revise — Phase landed was wrong for the glob feature: repro verified, census graph and query on a quoted glob all report 0 files while the directory form reports 95. Rescoped to active with one shared resolver in fs-walk.ts, fixtures first, then a probe. Verb names and aliases stay landed. Capabilities ownership left untouched.
 >>[2026-10-12 14:55] decide — User decided 2026-10-12 to start the shared glob resolver with the first three private walkers: census, graph and query. Other walkers follow after the resolver is proven. Glob engine still open: Node 23 here has fs.glob but the engines floor is 20.19 and 22.12 only gets an experimental fs.glob.
 >>[2026-10-12 14:57] decide — User decided 2026-10-12 to back the shared glob resolver with picomatch. Declared as a dependency of packages/spw-cli at ^4.0.7; it was only present transitively through vitest. No Node upgrade plan yet; the engines floor stays 20.19.
 >>[2026-10-12 15:37] revise — Commits named per the convention in 90c58bbc: 3 landed, 5 planned, 0 dropped.
 >>[2026-10-13 12:11] revise — Census on a quoted prompts glob still reports 0 files against 95. The caches name sense defects no plan held: census next frames print a roots placeholder, and census and graph never check anchors. Added next_fills_roots, census_checks_anchor, hole_selector, and two ranked study asks, outline_shows_omissions and predict_compare.
 >>[2026-10-13 20:52] observe — The owner wants to scaffold a skeleton and fill its details with an agent. On their tmp.spw scaffold, emit holes skipped an empty string, counted _ without naming it, inserted a fill unquoted, cut a default at its first closing brace, and ran its header into the output. holes_name_slots and holes_brief are planned beside hole_selector.
 >>[2026-10-27 10:21] measure — Glob gap rerun on 2026-10-27 at 850077ab: census on a quoted prompts glob still reports 0 files, against 95 files and 9205 lines for the directory form, so glob_fixture_tests stays next. Commit prose now says lens and focus marks for the #: and #! particles; the render study item numbers resolve through the render_study root.
 >>[2026-11-06 17:24] observe — A mounted consumer asked on 2026-10-25 (corpus-and-cli.spw, explicit_generated_paths, rank 7) that a path named on the command line be read even under .spw/gen/: spw lint on four of its generated route trees reported files=0, and it wrote its own parse-and-resolve pass for them (231 files, 5,759 citations). fs-walk.ts applies shouldSkipCorpusSurface to an explicit argument as it does to a walk. Planned as explicit_paths_walk; it belongs with the shared glob selection, which already decides what a named path means.
 >>[2026-11-06 17:24] observe — Three grammar censuses on 2026-11-06 (medial capsules by arm, line-crossing arms, operator-led capsules) needed one-off scripts: spw query --expr '$<_>' selects capsules and --count totals them, but --where placement=medial returned path-tag capsules unfiltered. Planned query_where_fields; reading-drift uses the same fields.
 >>[2026-11-08 09:47] observe — A second mounted consumer's review (2026-11-08) asks for verbs named for tasks: read (parse and print), check (strict parse, resolve refs, validate against a pinned schema, flag unread header keys; folding lint, doctor, integrity and format --check), eval <expr> --with data.json (no such verb exists), explain <expr> (gloss each clause and its source), audit <spell> (recompute derived clauses, run checks, verify a pinned policy) and one map (tree, roots, graph, census). Its rule: a verb stays when it has a named consumer. Filed as ?[task_verbs].
 >>[2026-11-09 18:12] observe — Measured reach by script on 2026-11-09 (journal 2026-11-09-distance): hops from the canon root, lines back to the nearest anchor, and marks to know before a line reads. Here 154 of 1,276 surfaces are unreached and nothing reports it; graph already speaks of spread as near, standard and far. reach_product planned for census and graph, and edge_stance opened: whether an unreached surface says it is arriving or leaving. wonder_trial_distance is homed here.
 >>[2026-11-09 18:13] observe — Tool gap found while measuring reach on 2026-11-09: run from a second mounted consumer, spw query --from .spw --selector pathRefs scanned 3 of its 84 surfaces and returned 16 rows, where a direct scan finds 313 path refs in 54 files. Its refs are written from the repository root and its index routes few surfaces by path ref, so the walk had nothing to follow; the scan count is the part to explain first. reach_product should report scanned against present, so a short scan is never read as a small corpus.
 >>[2026-11-09 19:12] observe — Reach from a centre, measured 2026-11-09 (journal 2026-11-09-distance, from_a_centre): walking the resolved refs from any held surface costs 0.1 to 3.2 ms; from a live plan the rings are 7, 29, 57 and 81 surfaces outward and 6, 16, 34 and 54 inward. Cycles: 13 groups hold 652 of 1,222 surfaces here, the largest being the living caches tree (361 surfaces in one group), and no journal entry sits in any. reach_product should take an origin: the canon root by default, any surface on request. wonder_trial_centre is homed here.
 >>[2026-11-13 16:49] observe — The surface at d30ebcb5, from spw help --json and each command's --help. 41 top-level verbs in five groups, and 25 alias routes. Bare spw and spw help print the same list, Workspace first; quest, the walk for a newcomer, is the fifth line of 41 and nothing marks it as the start. help --json carries name, group, summary, aliases and prose help for each verb, and no flag as data: an agent learns a flag only by reading prose.
 >>[2026-11-13 16:49] observe — A product is chosen two ways. form picks one of six products by flag (--static, --resonance, --field, --columns, --rhymes, the default card); inspect picks one of ten planes by subcommand (source, spacing, corpus, cache and six more); mem takes subcommands too. Output is chosen four ways across five verbs read: census and graph take --spw, --table, --json and --quiet; query and select take --format beside --table and --json; form takes --spw and --json and no table.
 >>[2026-11-13 16:49] observe — What machines write, counted with git grep for spw <verb> and spw:<verb> in this repo and two consumers, archives left out; prose that happens to say spw resolve counts too, so these are upper bounds. inspect 234, resolve 225, graph 184, census 158, query 147; at the other end mount 20, dev 14. Aliases: inventory, formulas, thrift, q, spwq and snip are written nowhere; fp, inv, stats, geom, experimental and catalog once or twice; topo 3. The alias policy in docs/runtime/spw/cli-command-surface.spw already lists a five-step retirement; with no human habit, an alias written nowhere has nothing left to migrate.
 >>[2026-11-13 16:49] observe — Of 56 npm spw scripts, 7 run a verb by an alias (spw:map, spw:invent, spw:seq, spw:analyze, spw:geometry, spw:surface, spw:skim), and the plan and agent scripts run bash, not the CLI. The readers added since 2026-11-12 have no verb: the spoken form, the brace sound and ear line, and the pair-label scan are reached only from code and from scripts/sound/pair-sound.ts; parse columns and rhymes are modes of form.

 >>[2026-11-13 17:00] implement — help --json gives every command its flags as data, read from the command's own help page so the two cannot disagree: names long and short, spellings, the value a flag takes, what it does, its section, and writes where its name says it writes. Flags a page names only in usage or prose are listed as mentions. spw <command> --help --json prints one command. Five pages that named their flags only in usage (atlas, tree, roots, snippet, beat) gained Flags sections; 272 flags are declared across 43 of 44 commands, and dev takes none.
 >>[2026-11-13 17:00] implement — Six alias routes written nowhere in three repositories are gone (inventory, formulas, thrift, q, spwq, snip); 19 routes remain, and spw measure thrift still reads as a family under measure. The 7 npm scripts named for an alias run the command by its name.
 >>[2026-11-13 17:00] implement — Three commands for readers that had none. spw say says each line in the names of its parts (spokenLine), with --clauses, --names site and --json. spw pairs lists labeled pairs, crossings and braces with no partner (scanPairLabels), and --at line:column gives the pairs around a place. spw hear writes a click per brace to a WAV file under an ear line; scripts/sound/pair-sound.ts is retired and npm run sound:pairs runs spw hear. Running say on crossed labels showed the spoken form naming a close by its open's label; it now names each close by the label the close carries.
 >>[2026-11-13 17:00] implement — Bare spw prints a start page read from the .spw tree it runs in, and spw help still prints all 44 commands. Outside a workspace it offers spw init. Inside, it says whether this is the workbench or a workspace that mounts it, suggests a walk through the workspace's own declared roots and index, lists the three surfaces changed most recently (tool-written folders left out), and ends with what reading the tree cost: 1,386 files in 173 ms here, 168 in 113 ms in a story consumer. A ^"start" frame in .spw/workspace.spw replaces the derived lines with the workspace's own, and recent: 0 skips the walk, leaving two files read.
 >>[2026-11-13 17:00] verify — Tests: flags read from every help page and named on it once per command; usage never read as a declaration; say matches spokenLine line by line; pairs reads labels, crossings, the unpaired and the pairs around a place; hear writes a RIFF file and reports an unknown ear word; the start page with no workspace, with derived lines, with recency ordered and tool folders left out, and with a ^"start" frame and recent: 0. Time on this machine: bare spw 1.35 s, of which 1.14 s is tsx starting and about 0.29 s is loading every command module before one runs.
}

